export const essays = {
  "guides/doberman-pinscher-health-issues.html": `<p>Fainting beside the food bin was how dilated cardiomyopathy introduced itself in the red male I fostered from the clinic kennel. He ate at noon and still looked sharp. By late afternoon his gums had gone dusty and his hind feet skated on the tile. That heart disease is the adult problem I build the year around. An echocardiogram is the heart check I book, first near birthday 1 while he still outruns the household, then again near birthday 2, and every year after those two baselines. At home the early picture is a softer cough, a stride that shortens on a familiar block, a collapse after the doorbell, or a dog who lies down halfway through a recall he used to finish.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck Veterinary Manual chapter on dilated cardiomyopathy</a> is what I send home when a family has never watched a heart fail in a dog who still wants to work. Bring the previous images if you switch clinics. A single normal year does not retire the next picture. I also count sleeping breaths for a week and keep the number in the phone, so a jump later has a comparison from this particular dog.</p>
<p>Bleeding that keeps seeping after a torn nail or a dental extraction is the home face of von Willebrand disease. I ask for a von Willebrand factor assay before any planned surgery, and I ask what the parents' results were. A routine chemistry panel can look ordinary while that clotting factor is the piece running low. Females in season and males who nicked a quick on a walk are the cases that taught me to ask early.</p>
<p>Wobbler syndrome, also called cervical vertebral instability, reads as a sway through the hindquarters, toenails that grind flat, and a neck the dog protects when a collar goes on. I request a neuro exam and imaging of the neck. Sore muscles after a long training day fade with rest. This pattern stays and often worsens. Hip dysplasia shows up on a different map: a bunny hop, a slow push up from the bed, or a refusal to climb into the car. Hip radiographs are the pictures I want on file for that pelvic picture.</p>
<p>Hypothyroidism is the quiet cluster. The sleek coat loses its gloss, the dog hunts warm spots, the same scoop starts to stick along the ribs, and the spark goes dull in a way an extra nap fails to fix. A thyroid panel is the bloodwork I order then. Cropped ears belong to US show custom. I have kept natural flaps and cropped leather, and medicine looks after whichever ear the dog arrived with. The crop is a ring habit. Cleaning and infection checks proceed on both shapes.</p>`,

  "guides/doberman-pinscher-temperament.html": `<p>Company is the piece he will not bargain away. Mine follows me into the bathroom, waits outside the shower, and claims the square where my shoes land. Leave him from breakfast until supper and the invented work starts: a shredded mail pile, a patrol along the fence, a bark that is really a request for a person. The breed was built to work beside someone, so the fix is a job small enough to finish and real enough to matter.</p>
<p>I set 1 tracking problem before breakfast. The leash gets hidden and he has to search the house until he brings it to my hand. After dusk I run 2 retrieve games in the yard, each one ending in a clean hand-off and a sit. He learns the edges of a task fast, then goes stale if I repeat a sloppy version. A protection-sport memory lives in the body, so I give that drive a legal outlet: a controlled bark on cue, a bite pillow with rules, a long down while the household moves around him. Absent an outlet, he polices the sidewalk on his own terms.</p>
<p>Harsh volume either shuts him down or spikes him. Clear markers and a job he can finish keep the same dog steady. Visitors get a serious once-over. After the introduction is done, he wants to be in the room where the people are, shoulder against my knee while I write charts from the clinic day. Affection shows up as contact and as watching my face, on his clock.</p>
<p>Puppies are softer and busier. Adults keep the same hunger for company and for real work. I still hand the adult a job every evening. A bored working dog hires himself, and the self-assigned work is rarely the work I wanted. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/doberman-pinscher/">AKC breed writeup covering Doberman working drive</a> matches the dog on my couch: made to do something beside a handler, restless when the something disappears.</p>`,

  "guides/doberman-pinscher-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Short sleek hair lies tight to the skin, so my hands do more than any tool. I start at the neck with a hound glove and make 3 slow passes down each rib, then over the croup, then along the thighs. Dust and a few needles of hair come up in the fabric. On day 5 of the week I check nails. A close coat makes a long nail obvious on hardwood, and those nails change how the foot lands.</p>
<p>Baths stay uncommon. Dirt shows on a slick jacket, and it also rinses fast. Mild shampoo, water until it sheets off, then a hard towel so damp clears the groin and the armpit. After a swim I inspect the ear leather on day 1 and again on day 2. Cropped rims and natural flaps trap moisture in different shapes. The rim gets wiped. The flap gets lifted so the canal can dry.</p>
<p>A rubber curry lives by the door. It lifts loose hair and knocks dried mud off without scratching. My bare palm follows the curry. Any bump, scab, or thin patch is easier to feel on this coat than on a fluffy one, and I want that map before a clinic visit. Tail, belly, and the inside of the hind legs get the same palm.</p>
<p>Lips get lifted while he is already standing for the glove. Feet get touched every session so nail day stays boring. Teeth and nails are part of the same sit for me, because a dog who only tolerates a brush will argue the clippers later. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/doberman-pinscher">VCA Hospitals care page for the Doberman Pinscher</a> lines up with what my hands find: a wash-and-wear coat that still needs a person who actually looks at the skin underneath it.</p>
<p>I keep a dab of canine toothpaste and a soft brush in the same caddy as the glove. Gumline tartar hides under a lip that looks clean from a doorway. Once a month I part the coat along the sternum and look for rash from a harness. The sleek look fools people into skipping the skin. The skin is the whole point of the grooming sit.</p>`,

  "guides/doberman-pinscher-exercise-guide.html": `<p>Work beside a handler was the original job, so the week I run is training plus companionship, with rest written in because the adult heart can fail while the dog still looks keen. Monday is 1 long block: heel, a retrieve, and a settle at the door, all in cool air. Then he rides along on errands so the company half of his nature gets met. Tuesday is 2 nose problems in the yard, short enough that he finishes wanting another turn.</p>
<p>Wednesday we use a quiet lot for position changes and a long stay while bicycles pass. Thursday is a leash walk with a destination, new streets, a chance to look, then a reorientation to my leg. Friday repeats a smaller set of Monday's skills. Saturday is play with rules, a tug that ends when I close my hand, a recall away from a good smell. Sunday is rest: a chew, a nap at my feet, no jumping repeats.</p>
<p>Heat or a hard heart day cuts the work. If he coughs, lags, or looks glassy, the session ends and the heart check moves up. I refuse to treat enthusiasm as proof the heart is fine. Young dogs get the same shapes with less duration while joints are still building. An adult with a current normal echocardiogram can do more, and I still keep one day that is only company.</p>
<p>A frantic fetch tires the body and leaves the mind itchy. A solved problem tires the mind in the way this breed actually needs. I aim for the second. Idle weeks fall apart: fence barking, counter raids, a dog who cannot settle when I finally sit. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/doberman-pinscher/">AKC account of the Doberman Pinscher as a working partner</a> is the page I show people who thought one loop of the block would cover the job.</p>`,

  "guides/doberman-pinscher-puppy-guide.html": `<p>The folder comes out before any cash leaves my hand. I want hip radiographs on the parents, a von Willebrand result, a thyroid note if the kennel ran one, and proof the breeding pair had heart checks. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC program packet for Doberman hip papers</a> is the stack I know how to read. A kennel that fumbles it is a kennel I leave. Ear cropping is a show-ring habit in the United States. I ask whether this litter still has natural flaps. I am buying a dog. The crop is optional theater.</p>
<p>Week 1 in my house is sleep, a crate beside the bed, and a leash to the grass. The body is legs and appetite. Week 2 the crate nap after meals is already boring, which is the goal, and I start hand-feeding so my fingers are part of good news. Through the early months play stays on grass. Long stairs wait. Weekend hikes wait. Growth plates are open while he races toward an adult frame. Males often finish 26 to 28 inches tall, carrying 75 to 100 pounds on a finished male. Females are aimed at 24 to 26 inches with a mass of 60 to 90 pounds. That endpoint is why a four-month clown does not jump out of the car alone.</p>
<p>Teething keeps the mouth busy. I trade a toy for the shoe every time and teach a settle on a mat. This breed will want a job. A mat settle becomes the earliest assignment. Company starts in the same week. A puppy exiled to the yard becomes a dog who cannot be still indoors later. He comes to the kitchen while I cook, to the clinic parking lot for one quiet sit, to the sidewalk for a single new sight, then home.</p>
<p>By the middle of the first year the chest is deepening and the clown is heavier. Meals stay scooped with a measuring cup. Ribs take a slight pressure to find. Heart screening belongs on the future calendar while he is still a puppy. I write birthday 1 and birthday 2 into the record as the first echocardiogram dates, the same adult plan his parents should already have been on. The sleek coat needs almost no work, which fools people into thinking the puppy needs almost no one. He needs a person and a task he can finish.</p>
<p>I also practice nail touches, ear lifts, and a stand for a fake exam before he hits forty pounds and the joke is over. Veterinary holds are easier when they were games. The first year is manners, measured food, and paperwork already in the folder, so the adult problems have a head start on the calendar instead of a surprise.</p>`,

  "guides/doberman-pinscher-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Hair leaves a short sleek jacket one stiff strand at a time, from the ribs, the neck, and the thighs. There is no thick undercoat blowing out in woolly clumps. What I find is short black or rust hair stabbed into fabric. It shows first in 1 seam of the couch, then along 2 baseboards where his hips rest while he watches the door.</p>
<p>Spring adds a little, and so does the week after a bath, when hairs that were sitting in the coat finally let go. A hound glove or a rubber curry pulls them while they are still on the dog. That is the only method I trust for staying ahead. Belly hairs are finer and paler. They drift onto dark shirts when he leans in. The tail leaves a ring of short hair on the car seat.</p>
<p>I once believed a coat this sleek would spare the laundry. The laundry told the truth. A white couch was a mistake I made one time. Rust hairs shout on charcoal upholstery. People hope dark fabric will hide them. It hides the black ones and advertises the rust. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/doberman-pinscher">VCA description of Doberman Pinscher coat type</a> calls the coat short, and short is why the shed is a scatter of needles instead of a tumble of fluff.</p>
<p>Vacuum the edges of rooms. He sleeps against walls, and that is where the jacket leaves evidence. A damp rubber glove run over the cushions lifts what the vacuum misses. I do this on Sundays after the curry, and the midweek pickup is smaller. The hair you see on the floor was growing from the outer jacket yesterday. It was never an undercoat.</p>`,

  "guides/doberman-pinscher-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>Families hear 10 to 12 years from me, and the next sentence comes in the same breath. Dilated cardiomyopathy is what pulls dogs under that span when the yearly heart check gets skipped or when the disease outruns the medicine. I have signed charts on dogs who chased a toy one month and were gone the next, the heart muscle thinned without a public warning. A baseline echocardiogram near age 1 and a repeat near age 2 give me something to compare when a cough shows up later.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck notes on heart disease in the Doberman</a> describe the failure I keep seeing: a big athletic dog whose pump weakens. Other problems shorten good years even when they spare the dog a single bad afternoon. Wobbler syndrome, that cervical vertebral instability pattern, can take the hind end and make a working dog miserable on his feet. Hip dysplasia does the same job from the pelvis. A bleed from von Willebrand disease can turn a routine dental into a crisis. Hypothyroidism, once a thyroid panel has named it, is the problem I can usually steady with medication, and a steady thyroid protects the gloss and the energy he uses to stay himself.</p>
<p>Pounds that creep up make the heart and the hips work harder. I keep the scoop honest and the waist visible from above. A July weekend of frantic fetching is an avoidable cut to a line that already reads 10 to 12 years. The dogs I watched reach the far side of that line had heart pictures on a schedule and a person who noticed the first cough instead of explaining it away as dust. I write that cough down the day it appears, even when breakfast still disappears on time.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-doberman-pinschers-get.html": `<p>Males I measure against the registry figures stand 26 to 28 inches. Their weight runs 75 to 100 pounds. Females stand 24 to 26 inches. Weight for them sits between 60 and 90 pounds. On one chart the pounds span a light female at 60 up to a heavy male at 100, and height can overlap where a tall female and a shorter male can share one withers number. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/doberman-pinscher/">AKC published height and weight for Doberman Pinschers</a> is the card I keep in the puppy folder so a photo stops being the measuring tool.</p>
<p>The youngster does not resemble that adult. At month 1 after he comes home the chest is narrow and the leg is most of the dog. At month 2 the appetite surges and the feet look too large for the body, which is the clue I trust. I write those weights down. Memory turns every pup into the biggest one in the litter.</p>
<p>By 12 months most of the height is up, and people declare him finished. The chest is still shallow beside the adult he will become, and the head is still refining. From 12 months to 18 months the sternum drops and the neck thickens. From 18 months toward 24 months the last muscle and the spring of rib fill in, males especially, sometimes pushing the top of that 75 to 100 pound band. Females often settle sooner inside the 60 to 90 pound band. Jumping stays low and meals stay scooped through that whole stretch so the frame finishes before a belly does.</p>
<p>A sleek coat makes ribs easy to misread. I want a waist from above and ribs that take slight pressure, on the male near 26 inches and the female near 24. Cropped leather or natural flaps do not change height at the withers. Withers means the top of the shoulder, dog standing square. I have watched a gangly dog at 12 months become a substantial dog at 24 months without gaining much height, only depth. That fill-out is what I describe when someone meets a pretty adolescent and thinks the final pounds are already on the scale.</p>
<p>Buyers who only met the sire on a grooming table misread height. I set a level at the withers and keep a hand under the chin so the neck stays neutral. Toes stretched for a treat can fake an extra inch, so I wait until he stands easy and then read the tape. The same animal weighed after breakfast looks heavier than he did before the walk, and both numbers go on one line with the date.</p>`,

"guides/boxer-health-issues.html": `<p>A stumble in the middle of a game is the call I get most about this breed. Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy is the muscle disease I name before anything else. At home you may catch a faint, a wobble, a strange thump under your palm on the chest, or a dog who ate breakfast and is down by supper. I book a Holter recording at age 1 and a second Holter at age 2, then whatever interval cardiology sets after those baselines. An echocardiogram still matters, because subaortic stenosis lives here too: a murmur at the left base, early fatigue, a heart pushing against a tight outlet.</p>
<p>Hips can develop dysplasia. After a nap he rises stiff, the rear hops, and the car jump gets refused. Films of the hips are what I order and then file. New skin bumps deserve more than a glance. Mast cell tumors mimic warts and bug bites. Lymphoma can appear as nodes under the jaw or behind the stifle that a photo from last month failed to show. Brain tumors can look like a seizure, a circling walk, or a personality that shifted over a fortnight. I want the lump aspirated. When the brain signs show, a neuro exam belongs in the same week.</p>
<p>Bloat is the night emergency. The abdomen goes tight as a drum, retching brings up nothing, and restlessness refuses to fade. That dog goes in immediately. Hypothyroidism looks quieter: a flat coat, a gain on the usual cup, a dog who seems dimmed. Thyroid bloodwork sorts it. A white coat carries a higher chance of deafness, so a white puppy gets a hearing test before anyone labels him stubborn. The muzzle runs longer than a Bulldog muzzle and still overheats, which means a July collapse might be heat, airway, rhythm, or all three at once. The <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck Veterinary Manual section on arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy</a> is the reference I trust when the rhythm talk starts.</p>
<p>I keep a written list on the fridge for the people who dog-sit: faint means emergency, tight belly with retching means emergency, new lump means a call the same day. Playing hard in humid air is a separate line on that list because the short face cooks faster than the joyful mood admits. None of this makes the clown less of a clown. It means the clown has a medical file I actually open.</p>`,

  "guides/boxer-temperament.html": `<p>The fawn male I kept into old age still broke into a play bow at year 10. At year 12 he was shoving a toy into my lap while I tried to finish clinic notes. Playful into adulthood is the temperament line I trust, clowning with adult muscle behind the joke. Joy knocks people over. I pay a sit before the greeting, and I end the tug while he is still offering another round.</p>
<p>Before lunch I count 50 rewarded nose-punches into my open palm. On the walk back I count 80 loose-leash steps between corners, then release him to sniff as the paycheck. Later there is 1 kitchen search, usually for a wooden spoon, and 2 yard games if the evening air is kind: a short wrestle with a stop cue, and a recall off the fence line.</p>
<p>He wants the middle of the group. A shut door makes him complain in a talking voice. Guests get the wiggle long before they get any caution, a greeting unlike a freeze-and-stare breed. Manners still have to be installed, because the adult clown is strong enough to unbalance a person. Harsh nagging produces more chaos. Focus shows up when the game stays fair and the pay is obvious.</p>
<p>Fawn or brindle, with white kept limited, is the color story under that personality. A blaze and a white chest are ordinary. A mostly white dog raises a hearing question I handle in the medical file, and the clown underneath can be the same clown. Heat changes the mood faster than any training plan. The short muzzle starts to snort, the pant gets loud, and the game ends on the cool floor whether I called the stop or he did.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/boxer/">AKC profile of Boxer play and character</a> reads like the room I live in. Busy, silly, attached to his people, and still a dog whose silly needs a channel or it becomes a ruined cushion. I give that channel every day he asks, including the gray-faced years, because the play does not retire when the muzzle goes white.</p>`,

  "guides/boxer-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Fawn and brindle pass under my mitt, white limited to a blaze, a chest stripe, or the toes. The hair is short and lies flat. Sunday gets about 10 minutes on the shoulders and barrel. Midweek, when dust settles into the dark stripes, I spend about 12 minutes and finish with a damp cloth so the white chest stays actually white.</p>
<p>I lay down 50 short strokes along the ribs and 80 strokes over the thighs, the shelves that collect yard grit. Nails are cut on a 1 week pace when the routes are mostly pavement, and on a 2 week pace when longer walks file them a little. I still look. Those nails curve and click before most people notice the sound on the kitchen floor.</p>
<p>A shallow wrinkle over the nose, on the dogs who have one, gets wiped and dried until the skin is matte. Salve piled in a fold is a habit I refuse. The same grooming sit is when lumps appear. Mast cell tumors can dress up as harmless bumps, so a new one triggers an aspirate at the clinic. A coat going flat and dull makes me think about thyroid bloodwork, and flea dirt in the groin gets a look on every pass.</p>
<p>Baths wait until he smells like the park pond. Shampoo, a long rinse, a towel, then a few minutes of walking so the armpits finish drying. Brindle hides mud until the mitt comes away gray. White markings advertise every stain. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/boxer">VCA Hospitals guide to Boxer coat care</a> fits a dog you can finish in a hallway with a mitt and a pair of clippers.</p>
<p>I also run a finger along the lip margins. Jowls hold a little moisture and a little breakfast. A wipe there keeps that smell off the couch. Teeth get brushed during that same stand, before he decides the session is over and starts offering a wrestle instead.</p>`,

  "guides/boxer-exercise-guide.html": `<p>Athletic and close to people was the old job, and the week I build is play, skill, and a rest day I actually enforce. The face is brachycephalic. Hot pavement ends the outing even when he is still begging for the ball.</p>
<p>Monday, cool air only: 50 minutes of grass fetch, then water and a real nap. I stop sooner if the pant turns raspy. Tuesday taught me a limit. 80 minutes became too much once the morning warmed, so that day is now two brief loops with the car cooled between them. Wednesday is thinking work, 10 retrieves to my hand and 12 changes from sit to down to stand, paid, then released. Thursday adds 3 steady holds on a low platform and 5 focus spins, used as a cue and stopped before anyone gets dizzy for fun.</p>
<p>Friday is 1 long shade walk where he picks the smells and I pick the turn toward home. Saturday allows 2 play invitations with a known dog when the temperature stays kind, with house breaks in the middle. Sunday is off. A chew, the couch, the ball put away in a closet. Playful into adulthood means he will ask on Sunday in his older years the same way he asked as a goofy youngster. The rest still happens.</p>
<p>All fetch and no thinking makes a frantic housemate. All rest and no play makes Monday explosive. That blend kept my own dogs looser indoors and kinder on their legs. Rain moves the thinking work inside: sits in the hallway, a search for a toy under a towel, stairs left alone. Collapse at any point stops the work until cardiology has spoken. Until then the sessions stay joyful and short enough that he ends loose and still interested.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/boxer/">AKC notes on Boxer energy in the home</a> describe the busy companion I plan around. A bored evening is when a cushion corner disappears. I spend the cool hour on a job he understands, and I spend less of it sewing foam. Summer outings move earlier. Winter outings can stretch. The airway holds the final vote over the calendar. When the pant stays easy and the gums stay pink, the next rep can happen. When either one changes, the day is finished.</p>`,

  "guides/boxer-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Money stays in my pocket until the file is open on the table. I want the parents' heart history, any workup aimed at arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy, any note on subaortic stenosis, and hip radiographs. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC paperwork for a Boxer litter</a> is the packet I can score in one sitting. A white pup in the group gets a hearing-test request before I fall for the face. Fawn and brindle with limited white are the colors I expect. A blaze is ordinary. A coat that is mostly white is the deafness flag.</p>
<p>Night 1 is a crate in the bedroom and a grass trip after every waking. Night 2 the crying shortens because the pattern already exists. Early months are sleep, play, and a mouth on the furniture. I trade a legal chew. Stairs stay rare, and I lift him in and out of cars. Males are headed toward 23 to 25 inches. Females are headed toward 21.5 to 23.5 inches. The square puppy thickens through the chest and the skull as the year turns. Scoops stay level so the joints haul bone and a soft belly stays off the plan.</p>
<p>The clown arrives early. I mark it with his name and a cookie, and I install a settle too, because playful into adulthood begins as playful in that crate. He parks under the table during dinner prep. Parking a puppy alone from morning until night is a plan I refuse. Heat and the short face mean summer outings stay brief while he is still all lungs and excitement. Teething outlasts the cute phase. Frozen washcloths help. Hands in the mouth now make adult tooth brushing possible later.</p>`,

  "guides/boxer-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Short hairs leave the jacket every month of the year. They match whatever jacket he wears, whether the body is fawn, whether it is brindle, plus white from the chest and the feet mixed through. I lift about 50 of them off a black shirt after a hug and about 80 off the passenger seat after one school run. A woolly undercoat is the wrong explanation. This shed is topcoat letting go of finished hairs.</p>
<p>In my house the spring increase lasts about 10 days. The fall increase lasts about 12 days, especially after a heat break when he picks a new sleeping wall. I see it first in 1 room, the one with the dark rug, and then in 2 more, the bedroom and the car. Brindle strands vanish on wood and shout on a white sheet. Fawn strands do the reverse. White chest hairs show on every background I own.</p>
<p>A rubber mitt before couch time removes a full palm. A bath loosens another wave about two days later. Groin and inner thigh shed a finer hair that sticks in the laundry hamper. The muzzle almost keeps its own hair. People who bought a short coat expecting an empty lint trap are the ones I walk through this with a shirt in my hand. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/boxer">VCA account of shedding from the Boxer jacket</a> matches that shirt test.</p>
<p>A roller lives in my work bag because my dog rides along. The pieces feel like grit, short enough to confuse with sand until you look close. That texture tells me the hair came off him. A long-coated patient who leaned on the same scrubs leaves a different thread entirely. I shake the bed cover outside before it goes back on, and the porch collects a little constellation of fawn that a broom actually picks up.</p>`,

  "guides/boxer-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>On the intake sheet I write 10 to 12 years, then I name the cuts. Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy can take a dog who still looked playful that morning. Subaortic stenosis can wear the pump down earlier than the family expected. Mast cell tumors, lymphoma, and brain tumors are the cancer pattern that removes a large piece of those years. Bloat can finish a life on one evening, belly swollen and retching with nothing to show for it. There is no slow week to negotiate. Hypothyroidism, once thyroid bloodwork has caught it, more often dulls the days than ends them on a Tuesday.</p>
<p>A heart check belongs on file by birthday 1. A habit of showing every new lump belongs in place by birthday 2. White coats raise the deafness chance. A deaf dog can live the whole span on hand signals, while an unheard car in the driveway is a separate risk I train around. Heat plus the short face can shrink a day and, on a bad afternoon, a life. Dogs I saw reach year 12 had someone who treated a faint as an emergency and a lump as a question for the lab. The <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck overview of Boxer cardiac disease and bloat</a> stays open on my screen during that conversation.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-boxers-get.html": `<p>Males finish between 23 and 25 inches at the shoulder. Females finish between 21.5 and 23.5 inches. Those are the registry heights I actually measure, the dog square on a firm floor, neck neither cranked up nor crouched. They are the only height figures I quote. Body condition I judge with fingers on the ribs and a look along the underline. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/boxer/">AKC height listings for male and female Boxers</a> are what I show a buyer who met a leggy pup and tried to guess the grown dog from a single glance.</p>
<p>Fill-out lags behind the calendar people expect. At month 1 in the new house I shoot a side photo and a front photo. At month 2 I repeat them, because the skull changes more in that stretch than memory keeps. Month 10 already shows the playful outline, chest starting to drop, paws looking less comical. At month 12 I log height with a real tape. At month 18 I log it again. At month 24 I close the growth notes. Plenty of males add the last of the chest between month 18 and month 24 while the shoulder tape barely moves. Females often lock closer to that 21.5 and 23.5 window a bit earlier.</p>
<p>Litters refuse to match each other. I have watched one pup run 50 days ahead of a sibling in chest depth and another sit 80 days behind on the same feeding plan. That gap is why a dog at month 12 is unfinished business. The head widens. The muzzle stays short. The body leaves its youngster square and becomes adult-athletic. White marks stay where they started. Fawn can deepen. Brindle can look sharper once the jacket lies tight against the rib.</p>
<p>Car jumps and long trail days wait until the month 24 picture looks stable. Portions stay scooped the whole way so muscle arrives ahead of a fat pad over the ribs. A short-faced puppy overheats faster than the height number suggests, so summer visits stay brief while bone is still stacking. I keep a weekly note of appetite and stool through those growth months. Sudden hunger with a soft waist is a portion problem. Sudden hunger with ribs still easy to feel and energy high is often the next growth kick.</p>
<p>Measuring tricks matter with this square outline. If he leans forward, the shoulder reads low. If someone tugs the tail into a show stack, the topline lies about him. I let him stand naturally, find the withers with two fingers, and drop the tape to the floor beside the front leg while keeping it off the space behind the paw. I read twice. When the two reads disagree, I throw both out and try on another day when he is less excited about the tape.</p>
<p>People line him up next to a Bulldog in their head and expect a heavier, lower animal. The outline here is up on leg. Between this face and a Bulldog face, the muzzle on this breed is the longer one. Pounds follow condition. I show the tape and the rib check when a puppy photo starts an argument. By the close of month 24 the dog in the hallway is the dog you are keeping: height settled, chest filled, clown still fully employed.</p>`,

"guides/miniature-schnauzer-health-issues.html": `<p>Fatty leftovers are the treat I refuse in this house, and I say so before any other topic. Pancreatitis and high blood fats travel together here. Bacon, a spoon of gravy, the fat drained off a roast: by night the dog is hunched, vomiting, and painful through the belly. I have watched that flare begin within 3 hours of the scrap and still have him hunched at hour 5. The test I request is a pancreatic lipase assay plus a blood-fat reading. Dogs living between 11 and 20 pounds have little room for a rich mistake. I still treat this in patients who have already reached year 12, and in patients who have reached year 15. Age hands out no free pass.</p>
<p>Straining on the grass, blood at the end of urination, or a dog who asks to go out and then stands there a long time points me toward calcium oxalate bladder stones. I request bladder imaging plus urine testing. These stones get missed when everyone is only talking about food. Fatty table scraps are already banned for the pancreas, so the bowl stays the diet the clinic prescribed. I leave blog recipes alone while the images are pending.</p>
<p>Myotonia congenita has DNA testing. A dog who stiffens when startled, marches a few toy-soldier strides, then loosens, is the picture I was taught to recognize. I want that result from the breeder before price talk, and I want it in the record of any adult whose gait looks electrical. Writing the full disease name in the chart keeps it from being filed as a quirky personality.</p>
<p>Night vision that fails, a pause on the dim stairs, or a bluish cloud in the lens sends me to ophthalmology. Progressive retinal atrophy is on the list for this terrier, and cataracts are on it too. An ophthalmic exam is the real test. A phone light in the kitchen is a poor substitute. I book the exam when signs show, and I also like a baseline while the eyes still look clear, because a later change needs a starting point.</p>
<p>Bumps lined along the spine are schnauzer comedo syndrome. They may stay quiet, or they may redden, itch, and get scratched open. The clinic should see them when they change, ooze, or the dog hates being touched there. Parting the jacket is how you see the row. A glance from the doorway misses it.</p>
<p>Beard drip and dusty eyebrows make people think the medical story is cosmetic. The story is the pancreas, the bladder, the muscle channel, the retina, and the skin over the back. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/miniature-schnauzer">VCA Hospitals medical overview of the Miniature Schnauzer</a> is the printout I hand families who arrived sure a small terrier only needed vaccines. A dog at 11 pounds and a dog at 20 pounds can share that list. Year 12 and year 15 are late enough that a first stone or a first pancreatic night still surprises people who thought the risky years were over.</p>
<p>What I ask owners to notice at home is specific. Vomiting plus a prayer posture means the pancreas until proven otherwise. Repeated squat-and-nothing trips mean the bladder until the images say otherwise. A startle that freezes the limbs means the muscle disease until DNA testing says otherwise. Bumping into furniture at dusk means the retina. A row of blackheads you can feel under the wire coat means the comedo syndrome. Those five pictures cover the breed better than a vague worry ever will.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-schnauzer-temperament.html": `<p>Alert is the default setting, and the bark is how he files the report. Leaves, trucks, a neighbor's latch, the refrigerator fan: each one can earn a bulletin. I teach quiet with 3 repetitions at the door and 5 more when a delivery truck grinds past, paying the silence and letting a single warning bark stand as the news. Erasing the voice fights the terrier. Giving the voice an assignment keeps the house livable.</p>
<p>Vermin were the original work. He still stares into shrubs, locks onto a scent along the fence, and will gut a toy that squeaks like prey. I use that focus. A carton with kibble buried in packing paper becomes the ratting game after dishes. He holds opinions about the rules. Too easy, and he invents a harder version, often involving the trash. Clear pay and a clean ending keep him from hiring himself.</p>
<p>Furnishings and the beard make strangers say cute. Under the eyebrows the brain is still terrier: quick, watchful, ready to argue a pointless repeat. Sharp scolding rolls off or turns into more noise. Brief clear reps land. He likes the middle of the action and he also likes a perch for supervising. Mine takes the sofa back when the street is busy and my ankle when the house goes quiet.</p>
<p>Salt and pepper, black, or black and silver changes only the look. The attitude stays put. I have kept all three colors, and the doorbell policy never shifted with the dye. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/miniature-schnauzer/">AKC temperament sketch of the Miniature Schnauzer</a> matches the supervisor on my furniture: sturdy little terrier, voice included, happiest when the day contains one problem he is allowed to solve.</p>
<p>Left without that problem he patrols windows and narrates the sidewalk. A tired brain from a search game sleeps. A tired body from frantic laps around the coffee table does not produce the same quiet. I choose the search. Guests who squeal at the beard get a dog who assumes the squeal is an invitation to climb them, so I put a sit in front of the hello and pay it every time.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-schnauzer-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Wire coat, plus furnishings on the beard, the eyebrows, the legs, and a skirt: that is the handful. Hand-stripping pulls dead jacket and leaves the harsh feel. Clipping is the route when a home wants a softer outline and a faster salon slot. I have done both on my own dogs. Stripping suits a jacket that was meant to feel like wire. Clipping suits a pet house that will keep the appointments. Either path still has to show the skin.</p>
<p>Beard combing happens 3 times between dawn and dark, always after meals, because water and food hang there and sour if I pretend they will not. Jacket work, strip or clip, lands every 5 weeks on my calendar so the body coat never felts. Eyebrows get shortened only enough that he can see. I keep a little fall and I refuse a shaved skull. Leg furnishings tangle behind the elbows. That spot is where I start when he is impatient, since a mat there changes the stride.</p>
<p>With the jacket parted I feel along the spine for schnauzer comedo syndrome bumps. Grooming is when I find them, before they are angry. Ears get a sniff and a look each session. Wire on the outside gives a dirty canal no excuse. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/miniature-schnauzer">VCA grooming outline for Miniature Schnauzer furnishings</a> matches the weekly reality in my bathroom: the cute parts are the parts that need hands most often.</p>
<p>Salt and pepper shows grime in the beard immediately. Black hides it until the odor arrives. Black and silver splits the difference. A small comb lives by the water bowl and another by the back door. Skip a month and the clipper has to rescue what a comb could have kept ordinary.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-schnauzer-exercise-guide.html": `<p>A ratter keeps the job even when the yard is suburban. Inside an 11 to 20 pound body he still wants the hunt. My week borrows that work and then schedules rest, so nobody asks a bearded little dog to be a distance athlete.</p>
<p>Monday is a sniff walk with a job in it, shrubs and stone walls, on a long line so he can work scent and still be stopped before the curb. Tuesday is a 12 minute search in the garage, boxes and kibble twisted in paper, a barn built from storage. Wednesday holds 3 recall games down the hall, each one ended before he gets bored and writes a new rule. Thursday stretches the search to 15 minutes because the garage is familiar and he needs a fresh corner. Friday is 5 minutes on a stable pile of boxes, then a nap. The climb scratches the earthdog itch without a real den that could trap him.</p>
<p>Saturday's longer outing happens only when the air is kind and the pavement is cool. Small bodies heat up fast and they chill fast too. I watch ears and belly. Sunday stays quiet: a chew, a lap, no performance. Those same games still fit a dog who is keen at year 12 and keen at year 15. A pancreatic flare or sore joints rewrites the minutes. The shape of the week stays.</p>
<p>Fence narration is what I get when the week contains no hunt. He can spend that energy on a box. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/miniature-schnauzer/">AKC description of Miniature Schnauzer ratting work</a> is the history I am copying, scaled to a hallway and a garage. Dogs at 11 pounds and dogs at 20 pounds both finish this plan tired in the brain. That is the tired that lets the house sleep.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-schnauzer-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Clearances come home with me ahead of any payment. I want DNA testing for myotonia congenita on the parents or the litter, an eye report that covers progressive retinal atrophy plus any cataracts, and the <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC folder for Miniature Schnauzer clearances</a> open so I can see whatever else the kennel already ran, orthopedic films included. A seller who points at a website instead of handing paper gets no deposit from me.</p>
<p>Week 3 in the house is when the beard wipe starts, after every meal, even while the chin hair is still short. Week 5 is when the shoulder coat starts to feel less silky and more like the wire it is headed toward. That change surprises people who expected plush forever. Stripping versus clipping can wait. Right now the work is tangles behind the ears and porridge in the chin hair.</p>
<p>Height finishes somewhere in the 12 to 14 inch span, and the weight finishes inside 11 to 20 pounds. The whole first year looks small, which tempts people into couch launches and stair races. Landings stay low. The bones are little and still building. House manners and a quiet cue start before barking becomes the hobby. I pay three warning woofs down toward one. The terrier opinion is already in the room. I spend it on tiny problems he can win.</p>
<p>Eyes get watched from the opening weeks. Hesitation in dim light sends us to ophthalmology early, ahead of any cloudy lens announcing cataracts. The pancreas rule starts at the bowl. Fatty scraps stay out, including holidays and training jackpots. Rewards are his regular food, or a piece lean enough that I would offer it to a dog already recovering from a flare. By the close of the year he should hold a settle, a recall, a comb he tolerates, and a file that already contains the DNA result.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-schnauzer-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Loose hair here is wiry, and it comes from named places: from the beard, from eyebrows, from leg furnishings, and from the jacket whether that jacket is clipped or stripped. This coat skips the twice-yearly wool dump of a double-coated northern dog. Dead jacket hairs sit until a stripping hand pulls them or clippers take them. The beard drops short crumbs every time he shakes after a drink.</p>
<p>I comb the beard and collect those crumbs across 3 passes, usually after breakfast while the furnishings are damp. Leg hair gives up a pinch on pass 5 of the weekly comb-out, especially behind the elbows where friction works the coat. Those are the hairs on a black shirt and in the bathroom sink. They are short, pale or black depending on color, and they feel like the bristles of a brush.</p>
<p>Salt and pepper leaves a mix that looks like ground pepper on pale tile. Black shows on the grout lines. Black and silver leaves both. A stripped coat drops less between sessions because the loose coat left during the strip. A clipped coat can shed a softer fuzz until the next haircut. Neither version blankets a sofa the way undercoat does. The evidence stays local: sink, shirt, the corner where he shakes.</p>
<p>Calling the breed shed-free and then blaming the cat is a conversation I end by setting the beard comb on the table. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/miniature-schnauzer">VCA explanation of the Miniature Schnauzer wiry coat</a> describes the jacket I am combing. Flakes from schnauzer comedo syndrome along the back are skin scale. That mess is separate from the wire hairs, and I keep the two talks separate too.</p>`,

  "guides/miniature-schnauzer-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>The range I trust at the clinic is 12 to 15 years. I say that line out loud, then I talk about what pulls a dog off it. Bodies sit around 11 to 20 pounds. On a frame that small, a pancreas that flares, along with blood fats that run high, is a real way to lose years, usually after fatty leftovers everyone called tiny. A bad flare can take an older dog across a weekend. Repeated milder flares steal appetite and comfort through the middle years. Calcium oxalate bladder stones are the other thief. Blockage, infection, and repeat procedures wear a small dog down even when each crisis gets handled.</p>
<p>Progressive retinal atrophy, together with cataracts, changes the life before either one ends it. A dog who cannot map the evening hallway gets hurt, gets anxious, and sometimes gets lost in a house he knows by daylight. I recheck eyes around year 3 and again around year 5 on my own dogs, stacked on the yearly visit, because a clear lens in youth is only a starting photo. Myotonia congenita is the answer I want from DNA testing before puppyhood even starts. A dog who locks when startled loses safe years of movement, and the stiffness is easy to misfile as attitude.</p>
<p>Schnauzer comedo syndrome rarely signs the last line on a chart. Chronic skin pain still shrinks a good life if the back stays raw. I count those as quality years, the same way I count a dog who can still work a box at year 12 and a slower dog who is still interested at year 15. The far side of 12 to 15 belongs to bowls that stayed boring, eyes that were examined, and breeders who had already run DNA testing for the muscle disease.</p>
<p>Staying inside that 11 to 20 pound window matters more than guests think, because a padded little terrier is harder on his own pancreas. Ribs stay findable under my hand. Fatty human food stays out even when a visitor insists a bite is meaningless. That bite is the story behind more than one chart I have closed early.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/miniature-schnauzer/">AKC longevity line for the Miniature Schnauzer</a> stays open beside me during this talk. I promise nothing about year 15. I do say year 12 is common when the pancreas, the bladder, and the eyes were on the plan from the start. A dog lost near year 5 to a crisis we could have avoided is the chart that still bothers me. Wire coat and a funny beard have nothing to do with how long the life runs. The daily bowl does.</p>
<p>I also watch water intake and squat frequency the way other people watch a cough in a bigger breed. Stones and kidneys announce themselves as bathroom changes long before anyone calls it aging. Catching that at year 3 or year 5 keeps year 12 available. Ignoring it makes the published span a number on a poster instead of a life in the house.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-miniature-schnauzers-get.html": `<p>The figures I use put the breed at 12 to 14 inches, with weight near 11 to 20 pounds, and males and females share the window. A male often sits toward the top of it and a female toward the bottom, though I have met plenty of exceptions and I refuse to treat sex as a guarantee. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/miniature-schnauzer/">AKC size window for Miniature Schnauzers</a> is the card on my clipboard when a buyer is guessing from a fluffy puppy picture.</p>
<p>I weigh at month 3, again at month 5, then at month 8, then at month 12. A lot of the height is visible by 8 months. Adult pounds usually settle by 12 months, with a little substance still possible afterward and almost no new height. Furnishings lie about size. Beard and leg hair add outline. After a bath, soaked to the skin, he looks like half the dog he was before the water. Coat is costume.</p>
<p>Month 15 is a hair story. The skeleton finished earlier. The beard can still be filling while the shoulder tape has been done since month 8. People who meet him then swear he grew. He grew coat. I part the jacket and show them the same dog. Puppy fat in the first half year is the other illusion. Ribs should be findable at month 3 and at month 5. A barrel on a puppy this size is usually the scoop, and it makes the grown dog look bigger than that published pound span was ever going to allow.</p>
<p>Reading the tape is fussier than it looks. He stands on his toes when he feels proud. I wait him out on a mat, feel the withers, and compare inches with the 12 to 14 listing. A reading of 11 inches at month 8 does not panic me. A reading still under the window at month 12 sends me back to the parents' cards. Weight gets the same patience. An adult at 11 pounds is inside the figure. An adult at 20 pounds is inside it. Past that, I look at condition before I hunt for a different breed hiding in the pedigree.</p>`,

"guides/bernese-mountain-dog-health-issues.html": `<p>Histiocytic sarcoma is the cancer this breed is known for, and it is the diagnosis I dread when a big tri-color dog comes in tired for no story that fits. Owners report lethargy, pounds falling off a dog who is still eating or who has quit food, a limp that switches legs, breathing that changes, or nodes that swell. I ask for chest films and a needle sample when that cluster shows. I quote no survival figures. I do say the workup should start when the signs start, because waiting for him to look like last month is how weeks vanish.</p>
<p>Hips and elbows both fail often enough that radiographs feel like ordinary adult paperwork to me. At home, hips look like a slow rise, a hop, a shorter stride on the usual lane. Elbows look like a head bob, a pause before the downstairs step, a leg that flips outward on landing. I want films of the hips and films of the elbows, read and stored. Anywhere from 70 pounds to 115 pounds, a dog can hide a limp inside a heavy coat and a polite face until the muscle wastes.</p>
<p>Bloat is the same-day emergency I describe with my hands on my own belly: the abdomen drums tight, he retches and brings nothing up, he cannot get comfortable. A deep chest, a big meal, and stress can stack into a twist. The <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck Veterinary Manual page on canine bloat</a> is reading I want finished before anyone needs it at midnight. Go in. Waiting out a stomach that looks wrong is how I have lost the hour that mattered.</p>
<p>Degenerative myelopathy arrives in the hind end as scuffing, knuckling, a sway, nails worn flat on the top surface. It lives in the spinal cord. A neuro exam is the first test I request. I keep a symptom log and I review a vague lameness at week 6 and again at week 8 when it has failed to declare itself as a cut paw or a sore muscle. Seeing 1 swollen node, or watching 2 days of skipped meals in a dog this size, is enough for me to call. Coat color and a sweet expression leave the medical chart untouched. The chart is cancer, joints, bloat, and the cord.</p>
<p>Heat sits on top of the whole list. A draft dog built for cool farm mornings overheats on a summer patio, and an overheated dog is harder to read when I am also worried about the belly or the lungs. Summer movement belongs in the morning, water stays poured, and any sudden collapse is an emergency until bloat and heat are both ruled out. Affection makes these dogs endure quietly. They lean while something inside is already failing. Believe the lean when it arrives with a limp, a cough, or a belly that feels like a drum.</p>`,

  "guides/bernese-mountain-dog-temperament.html": `<p>Draft work and farm chores beside a person are the job this dog still expects. Mine leans his whole chest into my legs while I finish charts, then follows to the door as if the next task might involve a cart. Affection is physical and a little heavy. He wants the room, the foot, the hip. Left alone in a yard from breakfast to dark, he pines or he booms. Company belongs inside the work. Treating it as optional misses the breed.</p>
<p>I offer 1 pulling game with a light cart or a dragging line on cool mornings, enough to scratch the draft memory without pretending a pet is a freight team. Then I offer 2 calm passes along the barn aisle or the hallway, him at my side while I carry a bucket, because walking while a person carries something is the farm picture I can still build. Tone sticks. A sharp correction lingers on his face for the rest of the afternoon. Clear cues and a soft voice get more work out of him than volume ever pulled.</p>
<p>Heat rewrites the mood by early afternoon in summer. He hunts tile and declines the game he loved at dawn. I believe the decline. Forcing a farm-dog noon in July produces a dull, panting partner and teaches nothing I wanted. Visitors are usually greeted as new people to lean on. The lean of a dog this size can pin someone against a cabinet even when the intention is love, so the sit happens before the hello.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/bernese-mountain-dog/">AKC portrait of the Bernese Mountain Dog farm partner</a> describes a dog who wants a task and a person inside the same hour. Supply both and the house stays peaceful. Skip the task and the person-need remains, coming out as shadowing, sighing, and a paw parked on your knee while you try to type.</p>`,

  "guides/bernese-mountain-dog-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Tri-color double coat is a literal handful. I line-brush and part to the skin along the croup, through the neck ruff, down the pants, and to the end of the tail. Guard hairs are long and slightly oily. Undercoat is the dense layer that mats when I only skate over the surface. Ear leather under the flap gets a look every pass, because feathering hides a sore.</p>
<p>Baths land every 1 to 2 months unless feathers are dragging mud or he found the creek. Drying is the actual labor. Undercoat against the skin stays wet long after the outside looks fluffy. I towel, then I push air through until my fingers at the skin feel dry. Leg feathers and the tail get combed while they are damp so they dry as separate strands. A mat that dries first becomes a project. A mat combed wet becomes a comb-out.</p>
<p>Coat blows change the tool. An undercoat rake earns its place in spring and again in fall, used gently so loose undercoat leaves and the guard coat stays. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/bernese-mountain-dog">VCA coat care notes for Bernese Mountain Dogs</a> fits the dog in my tub: handsome, heavily coated, and miserable if I rush. Nails hide inside feathering. I find them by feel. Teeth get brushed during the same stand, once he has already accepted that we are staying put.</p>`,

  "guides/bernese-mountain-dog-exercise-guide.html": `<p>The week follows farm work and draft work, scaled to a companion who overheats and who carries a lot of bone. Monday is 1 easy loop with a little wagon, or with a harness that only drags, down a cool lane, then a drink and shade. Tuesday is 2 field walks wearing a light pack, him beside me, stops whenever the pant turns loud. Cutting Tuesday in half beats spending the next day watching him skip dinner from heat.</p>
<p>Wednesday adds 3 direction changes and 5 pauses to stand still, practiced where the footing is kind. Those pauses are the job. A draft partner who can wait while a gate opens is doing the real work. Thursday is scent play in the shade, finding a glove or a feed scoop. Friday repeats a shorter cart only if the air stays cool. On a hot Friday the job becomes a sniff indoors and a brushing, and I count it. Saturday can be a longer trail when the forecast is kind and he is a sound adult. Sunday is a floor day: a chew, no harness, no heroics.</p>
<p>Growing dogs skip cart weight and long trails. Their version is grass manners and short beside-you walks. Joints are building under a body that will be heavy. Adults who already limp keep the company and the brain work, and the distance drops. Heat vetoes every day, including the pretty ones. A farm dog from a cool valley owes nobody a noon hike.</p>
<p>Empty weeks show up as shadowing that turns worried, a bark at a nothing-sound, a dog who paces the kitchen because the outdoors never happened. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/bernese-mountain-dog/">AKC working history for the Bernese Mountain Dog</a> is the job description I trim to a modern lot and a hot climate. One person, a task, a cool hour. That is the whole exercise idea. Snow treats him more kindly than July does, and a plowed lane with the wagon beats any attempt to jog him at dusk in August.</p>`,

  "guides/bernese-mountain-dog-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Joint papers are in my hand before any deposit moves. I want radiographs of both parents' hips and of both parents' elbows, and I ask which relatives were lost to histiocytic sarcoma. No form erases that cancer. I still want the honesty. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC orthopedic file for Bernese parents</a> is the stack I read for the joints. A breeder who cannot produce it can talk to someone else.</p>
<p>Month 1 at home is grass, a leash, and sleep. The bone already feels heavy while the dog is still small, and the growth ahead is large. Males are pointed toward 25 to 27.5 inches and 80 to 115 pounds. Females are pointed toward 23 to 26 inches and 70 to 95 pounds. Month 2 adds a settle beside the desk, because this breed wants someone nearby while those joints are still soft. Stairs, launches off furniture, and weekend hikes stay off the list for the entire first year.</p>
<p>The coat comes in thicker. What other breeds show as a wild clown, this one shows as a leaner and a follower. Social time stays calm: one new surface, one friendly visitor, then home. Over-tired pups get rude. Portions are measured so a rapid gain avoids stacking mass on open plates. Ribs stay findable under the fluff. Draft games wait until the frame is actually grown. The opening year is for being a puppy next to someone, parents' films already filed.</p>`,

  "guides/bernese-mountain-dog-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Hair leaves a tri-color double coat. Volume is the whole subject. Guard hairs drift a little all year from the ruff, the rear pants, the tail, and the backs of the ears. Undercoat is what blows. In spring at my place the blow runs about 6 weeks. In a damp fall it has run about 8 weeks. Clumps come off in my fingers when a rake reaches the skin. From 70 pounds up to 115 pounds, that undercoat is a lot of dog, and the hallway proves it.</p>
<p>Tumbleweeds show up in 1 corner of the kitchen first, the corner where he circles before lying down, and then in 2 corners of the mudroom. Black hairs announce themselves on pale tile. Rust hairs announce themselves on the bath mat. White hairs from the chest and toes show up on every dark piece of clothing I own. Pants and tail hold the longest strands. Belly undercoat is softer and exits in grayish tufts when the stomach gets skipped during a brushing sit.</p>
<p>A bath during the thick of a blow loosens an alarming mass, and the rinse water looks full of felt. I rake before the bath, bathe, then rake again once he is dry. Between seasons the shed calms and people decide it ended. It only quieted. Guard hairs keep drifting onto the car seat, shorter than the spring clumps and easy to ignore until black trousers are furry at the knee.</p>
<p>This is the heavy seasonal shed of a working coat, the coat a draft dog wore for cold weather. Indoor heat can stretch the blow or dull it. I still plan on spring and fall as the serious stretches. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/bernese-mountain-dog">VCA description of Bernese Mountain Dog double coat shedding</a> matches the bags I fill. Brushing only the top leaves undercoat in place to mat, and the mat later leaves in a sheet. Reach the skin. That is the layer the hair is actually quitting.</p>
<p>I keep a laundry basket by the back door during those weeks and I empty the dryer lint trap every load, because the trap fills with black and rust in a way it never does in January. The white blaze hairs twist into the lint and look like cotton. If I skip two days of raking, the corners of the room grow soft piles that roll when the door opens. That pile was undercoat this morning.</p>`,

  "guides/bernese-mountain-dog-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>The line I give families is 7 to 10 years, and in clinic the dogs often land toward the short end of it. I have closed more records near year 7 than near year 10. Histiocytic sarcoma is why I say that plainly. The cancer takes dogs who still look as if years remain, sometimes after a limp or a tired month that seemed ordinary. Bloat can erase a dog on one night. Degenerative myelopathy can steal the rear legs and shrink what the remaining time feels like. Pain from hips and from elbows does the same quiet shrinking.</p>
<p>A photo from month 6 of the first year, and another from month 8, stays beside the later notes. The puppy who looks indestructible is the same animal whose line runs 7 to 10 years. Families sometimes describe the first serious illness on the early side of that span. I also want bloat signs known before year 1 of adulthood and refreshed at year 2, because an emergency recognized at midnight is a different night from one spent searching a phone.</p>
<p>Nothing about the tri-color coat lengthens the span. Cool work, a body that stays leaner, joint films before any breeding, and a fast response to a tight belly are the practical pieces. They fail to turn 7 to 10 into some longer promise. They give the short end a better chance of being good time. <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck discussion of gastric dilatation in deep chested breeds</a> is the reading I hand over when someone thinks bloat belongs to a different shape. This deep chest is exactly the shape I worry about after dinner.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-bernese-mountain-dogs-get.html": `<p>Males are listed from 25 up to 27.5 inches. They carry 80 to 115 pounds across a typical male. Females are listed from 23 up to 26 inches. They carry 70 to 95 pounds. I treat the distance from 70 pounds on a light female to 115 pounds on a heavy male as the full window I might meet, with most dogs landing somewhere honest inside it. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/bernese-mountain-dog/">AKC height and weight ranges for Bernese Mountain Dogs</a> stops the guessing that starts when a fluffy pup at eight weeks is the only dog a buyer has stood next to.</p>
<p>During month 1 the puppy is already substantial in bone and still nothing like the adult mass. During month 2 the coat starts to hint at the grown outline while the legs do most of the visual work. At month 6 the legs look long and the body looks light for those paws. At month 8 the chest begins to drop and the head widens. By 12 months people want to call him finished. He is tall enough to fool a camera and still light through the rib and the thigh.</p>
<p>Between 18 and 24 months the adult mass shows up. Males especially fill chest, neck, and hindquarter and may climb toward the top of the 80 to 115 band. Females often settle inside 70 to 95 with less drama, though I have watched females take the full stretch too. I log a weight during month 1 and during month 2 so the curve has a beginning, then again at 12, at 18, and at 24. Jumps and long hikes wait through that fill. A heavy puppy becomes a heavier adult with sore joints, and this breed already spends joint health at a high price.</p>
<p>Coat lies about the ribs. I part fur and use a flat hand. Ribs should be findable, and I want a waist I can see when he is wet or when the undercoat has blown. Dry, fully coated, standing in winter, almost every one of them looks larger than the scale. Read height on a firm floor at the withers. The top of the ruff is theater and it adds no inches.</p>
<p>Buyers ask me whether a bigger pup is a better one. I want the pup whose parents sat inside those ranges and whose own curve stays steady. A youngster already looking like a full male at month 8 is one I watch at the bowl. The adult should arrive by 24 months looking fit for the old job: substantial, able to move, coat heavy, and still light enough on his feet to walk a lane. That is the size I accepted when I brought the breed home.</p>`,

  "guides/shih-tzu-health-issues.html": `<p>The face is where trouble shows first on this toy. Facial hair rubs the cornea, so the eye corners get cleaned every day, and on a windy day I clean them 3 times. If we were outdoors I look again around hour 5. Redness, squinting, or a thick strand of discharge means wiping is no longer the whole answer. A blow to the face can push the globe forward. That displacement is proptosis, and it goes straight to the clinic. I support the head, keep other animals back, and I leave the eye alone. Sticky discharge with a dull surface makes me request a tear-production test. Keratoconjunctivitis sicca is the dry-eye name, and hair hides it until someone actually looks.</p>
<p>Ears sit next to the eyes on my list. Head shaking, a yeasty smell, and rubbing an ear along the rug are the signs I trust. Hair at the canal holds damp. I want a scope in that ear before anyone guesses the ointment. The back is the worry after the face. Intervertebral disc disease shows as a yelp when lifted, a hunched spine, a refusal to hop onto furniture, or legs that wobble. A neuro exam comes first, with spinal imaging if the signs are loud. <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck Veterinary Manual entry on intervertebral disc disease</a> is the page I want recognized before a leap off the bed becomes the crisis.</p>
<p>A skip in the trot, a hind leg held up for a few beats, then a normal stride again: that is luxating patellas at home. Palpation of the knee is the test, done standing and again while he is loose. Renal dysplasia shows earlier than families expect. A youngster who drinks past anything the bowl size explains, who grows poorly, or who loses house training that had been solid, needs kidney bloodwork plus urine testing. Bodies of 9 to 16 pounds can carry this whole list. A life running 10 to 18 years still needs those checks.</p>
<p>Heat belongs in the same talk because the muzzle is brachycephalic. Panting that continues inside a cool room, a noisy inhale, or a dog hunting the floor vent is a stop. I move air, I offer water, and collapse is an emergency. Small fails to mean sturdy in the airway or along the spine. The everyday eye wipe is the cheapest prevention I know. It is also how I notice the morning the eye looks different from yesterday.</p>`,

  "guides/shih-tzu-temperament.html": `<p>Companionship is the entire job. Mine shifts rooms when I shift rooms, treats a lap as a real assignment, and sighs when I stand up too soon. Attachment is the point. It can also become a protest at the door if a short goodbye was never practiced. I practice those. A dog this size still holds opinions, especially about handling, and the opinion looks like a planted body and a turned head.</p>
<p>He may weigh 9 pounds or he may weigh 16, and the spirit does not shrink with the scale. I have watched them stay playful and bossy from year 10 onward, and a fair number stay themselves out toward year 18. Play lives indoors: a soft toy, a wander down the hall, a comment at the window. Doorbells get announced. I teach 3 quiet greetings when the bell rings, plus 5 settle cues on the couch, paying the calm and letting one tiny woof count as the bulletin.</p>
<p>Heat changes behavior before it becomes a medical scene. He quits, seeks tile, and can turn cranky if the walk keeps being insisted on. I read that as news from a brachycephalic face. Summer evenings are laps and food puzzles. Mornings can hold a stroll. Visitors are usually welcome when the approach stays polite. A lap dog can still nip a rude hand on a sore ear, so guests learn the chin scratch he actually enjoys.</p>
<p>Long coat and a topknot make people use a squeaky voice. I use a normal cue, the way I would with any dog who knows one. That respect keeps the stubborn streak from turning every brushing into an argument. He will also plant himself in a doorway until the lap is offered again, which is companionship with a negotiator's streak. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/shih-tzu/">AKC companion portrait of the Shih Tzu</a> matches the shadow at my heel: small, close, and fully convinced the furniture is a shared project.</p>`,

  "guides/shih-tzu-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Length is the coat, and the topknot is a tool with a purpose beyond looks. Before the band goes in I wipe the inner eye corners so grit stays off the cornea through the remaining hours. Keeping facial hair off that cornea is the band's whole job. I retie it 3 times across a long day when he rubs his face on the rug or on my sleeve. A slipped topknot puts hair back in the eyes.</p>
<p>A full comb-through is 5 sections on my hands: head, ears, body, legs, and tail. I part to the skin and finish a small zone before I move. Skating over the surface leaves mats against the body that stay invisible until they tighten. Behind the ears and under the front legs are where felting starts. The sanitary area and the feet get trimmed short enough to stay clean and to reveal nails. Feathered feet grow nails in secret.</p>
<p>Ears need air. I thin the entrance carefully and I dry them after every bath. A soaked ear under a heavy fall of coat invites infection out of sight. Bathing has to be complete, and drying has to be more complete. Damp length against the skin will sour. He walks around, or moving air runs, until the roots feel dry. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/shih-tzu">VCA coat and face care notes for the Shih Tzu</a> line up with the time this actually consumes. A long-coated toy asks for that time. The time is the care.</p>
<p>Shorter pet trims help some houses. A shorter body still needs the face handled, because the eye problem is hair touching the eye, and a topknot or a face trim has to solve it each day. A comb lives in my kitchen. Food in the beard becomes a mat by evening when the after-meal wipe gets skipped. Coat color changes nothing about the order of work. Length does.</p>`,

  "guides/shih-tzu-exercise-guide.html": `<p>Companion steps replace mileage. The face is brachycephalic and the back can suffer intervertebral disc disease, so the week stays short, cool, and low. Monday is 3 laps of a shady block, slower than my natural pace, with time to sniff. Tuesday is indoor play, 5 soft tosses of a plush toy across a rug, then done. Leaping, twisting, and stair repeats stay out of the plan.</p>
<p>Wednesday is his kibble scattered for sniffing across the living room floor. Thursday repeats the shady block when the air stays kind. Friday is a quiet outing, a ride or a new room, then home. Saturday can stretch a little, only in cool weather and only on grass or dirt. Sunday belongs to a lap. Rest protects the airway and the discs. Summer noon stays indoors. I listen to the inhale. A noisy, effortful breath ends the walk even if the driveway is as far as we got.</p>
<p>A bored toy companion still needs a brain. The scatter and a flat-floor game, a sit and a spin without a jump, cover that without pounding the spine. Puppies get even less ground. Adults with a disc history get the indoor version only. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/shih-tzu/">AKC activity notes written about Shih Tzu companionship</a> fit a dog whose work is being near you. Near you can include a walk. A mile is a different animal's assignment.</p>`,

  "guides/shih-tzu-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Papers, then the face. I want an eye report, a patella check, and any kidney notes the breeder kept, because renal dysplasia can show while a dog is still young. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC papers requested for Shih Tzu breeding dogs</a> are the pages I can score without a translator. A seller who has never heard of a tear test or a knee exam gets thanked and left.</p>
<p>Grown dogs land between 9 and 16 pounds and stand about 9 to 10.5 inches, and many live from 10 years out toward 18. The puppy year is a long runway into that life, and it looks tiny the entire time. At week 3 I start the eye wipe, gently, at the inner corner, so facial hair never becomes a habit of scraping the cornea. At week 5 I introduce a soft topknot band for a few minutes, then off, so the band is boring before the coat is long enough to need it through a whole day.</p>
<p>Growth stays modest. Weights get written down so a puppy who is failing to gain, or who drinks as if the bowl had no bottom, gets kidney bloodwork early. Luxating patellas are something I ask the clinic to feel at vaccine visits, a skip or a pop caught while it is still small. Stairs and furniture jumps stay banned. I do the lifting. Discs and kneecaps get one childhood without impact games.</p>
<p>Puppy fluff gives way to longer adult hair, and mats start behind the ears before anyone expects them. Those spots get combed once the hair passes the leather. House manners, a lap settle, and patience for hands on feet are the skills for this year. Heat is already a limit. Brief cool outings, then home. By the first birthday the face should be clean, the folder should hold the parents' notes, and the body should still be light for a future somewhere between 9 and 16 pounds.</p>`,

  "guides/shih-tzu-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Floor hair here comes from a long coat. Most of what I pick up is broken ends, and there is no seasonal wool dump the way a northern double coat blows. Those ends leave the topknot, the ear fringes, the beard, the leg feathers, the skirt, and the tail plume. A comb pulls them during a grooming sit. Ignored, they tangle into little ropes and then snap off. Even at 9 pounds, and still at 16 pounds, the coat can reach the floor, so the supply of stray strand is bigger than the dog looks.</p>
<p>Across a life that may run 10 years or run 18, the coat keeps offering loose hairs when it is ignored, and it still offers them when it is cared for, because the comb itself removes dead length. A dark shirt often catches the pale broken tip from an ear fringe. The tub after a bath holds a nest from the skirt. The part line down the back gives the comb a finer felted thread.</p>
<p>I divide the coat into 3 parts on a short day and into 5 parts when the job is complete. Each part leaves its own dust on the towel. This is long hair breaking, loosening at the root, and catching on furniture until someone collects it. A pet trim lowers the volume because less length remains to break. Face and ears still contribute. A dog kept in a topknot and a skirt will leave evidence. That evidence is the coat doing what long hair does.</p>
<p>People expect a low-shed lap dog and then find hair wound through the vacuum brush. The texture gives the source away: longer than the needles off a smooth jacket, softer than wire, often slightly wavy. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/shih-tzu">VCA account of loose hair in the Shih Tzu coat</a> describes the same strands I pull off the bathroom rug. Tear stain on the eye corners is pigment on hair. That is face care sitting beside the shed, and washing the hair keeps the two from being one confused story.</p>
<p>Rooms where he shakes are the rooms that collect. After a bath the shake throws broken ends onto the towel and the nearby wall. Between baths, the sofa arm where the topknot rests picks up a ring of hair. I wipe that arm when I retie the band. Knowing the source stops the hunt for a mysterious second pet. The skirt was the second pet.</p>`,

  "guides/shih-tzu-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>The span I quote runs 10 to 18 years. The width surprises people who think every small dog lives forever and people who think every brachycephalic dog dies young. Both pictures are too tidy. Weighing anywhere from 9 pounds up to 16 pounds, these dogs can reach the far end when kidneys stay quiet, the back is spared jumps, and the airway gets respect in hot weather. They can also leave early. The charts I remember are usually renal dysplasia or intervertebral disc disease.</p>
<p>Renal dysplasia can shorten life from youth. A dog who drinks and drinks, who grows poorly, or whose bloodwork shows tired kidneys, may have fewer years, and the years that remain need a real plan. Disc disease can end comfort even when a heartbeat continues. Paralysis, pain, and a back that cannot tolerate an ordinary house change what a long remainder would have meant. I revisit kidney values around year 3 and around year 5 in dogs who drink more than littermates did, and I revisit them any year the water bowl empties faster.</p>
<p>Eyes change the quality of the span. Keratoconjunctivitis sicca, corneal scratchiness from facial hair, and proptosis after a bump can cost vision or the globe itself. Life can go on with one eye. Infection and pain are what shrink the good part of the years if the daily cleaning gets dropped. Chronic ear disease is the slow drain: repeat infections, head pain, a dog who stops wanting hands near the face. None of that has to be dramatic to steal comfort from a line of 10 to 18 years.</p>
<p>Heat stroke is the sudden cut. A brachycephalic muzzle on a summer patio can undo a dog who was aimed at the far end. I count that as avoidable. Luxating patellas rarely sign the final note, yet the arthritis that follows a popping knee makes later years smaller. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/shih-tzu/">AKC year span listed beside the Shih Tzu profile</a> is the range I explain when I talk about 10 years as something I see often and 18 as something I have seen in dogs whose backs, kidneys, and faces were looked after.</p>
<p>I promise nothing about the far number. I do line up the habits that belong with a long life here: eye corners cleaned, no diving off couches, water intake noticed, walks moved to the cool part of the day, ear odor handled early. Whether the scale says 9 pounds or it says 16 pounds, the talk stays the same. Where they sit inside that band sometimes changes a medicine dose. It leaves the list of what shortens the years unchanged.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-shih-tzus-get.html": `<p>Males and females share one window: about 9 through 10.5 inches at the shoulder, and a weight from 9 up to 16 pounds. I keep the sexes on one card because the figure I use does the same. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/shih-tzu/">AKC measurements for Shih Tzu height and weight</a> are what I show when a buyer is sure the fluffy pup will remain teacup-tiny. Some stay near the low end. Some fill toward 16 pounds and feel surprisingly solid once the coat is soaked flat.</p>
<p>The scale comes out during month 3 and during month 5, then during month 8 and during month 12. Height is largely set by 8 months. The number on the scale tends to level off by 12 months, and the coat keeps lengthening after the skeleton stops advertising new size. That explains how a dog at the one-year mark can look bigger later while the scale barely moves. Hair supplies the theater. A bath that wets every strand down to the skin reveals the actual small dog, which is the dog under the tape.</p>
<p>Growth is steady and easy to overfeed, because every added ounce shows on a frame this size. Even under fluff I should be able to feel ribs during month 3 and during month 5. I part hair to check. A round belly and a waddle are the scoop, and they make knees and backs work harder afterward. Landings stay low through this whole stretch. Adult height will not reward a puppy career of launching off furniture.</p>
<p>The tape takes patience. Curiosity makes him stretch tall. Tiredness makes him sink. I wait for a natural stand on a mat and then find the withers through the topknot, reading the 9 through 10.5 inch mark. Contentment for me is month 8 still approaching that mark and month 12 arrived inside it. Weight from 9 pounds to 16 pounds gets the same patience. Under the low number, I look at the parents, the teeth, and the appetite. Over the high number, I look at the bowl before I look for a mystery in the pedigree.</p>`,
};
